From 9c268ceeb3a2500e4b4ef7414c915b2e2c75b7ea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NanoLLM <nanollm@localhost>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 15:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] llama: flash attention on small batches only
 (LLAMA_FLASH_ATTN_TYPE_SMALL_BATCH), the matmul attention for long ones

A new llama_flash_attn_type value. The KV cache keeps the flash-attention layout; batches of up to 8 tokens
(decoding, the verification of speculative drafts) and the sliding-window layers keep flash attention, and a longer
batch on a full-attention layer goes through the matmul attention (KQ, softmax, KQV) with V transposed for it. It
needs an unquantized cache; with a quantized one it is plain flash attention. The sliding-window layers stay on
flash attention because it skips the masked blocks of a full-size SWA cache, which the matmul attention would
compute in full. common's -fa and llama-bench's -fa take small_batch.

The scalar flash-attention shader of the Vulkan backend is slow on an Iris Xe (no matrix cores, Windows driver
32.0.101.7092) as the context grows: pp512 drops from 475 tok/s at an empty context to 331 at 4096 and 252 at
8192, while llama-bench -fa 0 gives 462 / 409 / 355. None of the shader's host-side parameters closes the gap
(block rows 8/16/32, block columns 32/64, workgroup 128/256, D split 4/8/16, row split 1/4, subgroups off/16/32,
shared-memory staging: all within the noise or slower).

i7-12700H Iris Xe, llama-bench pp512, flash attention -> small_batch, tok/s:
- swa_full off (llama-bench's default): empty context 453-458 -> 433-455, at 512 408 -> 431, at 1024 368 -> 421,
  at 2048 371-373 -> 405-418 (+12%), at 3072 339-342 -> 385-402 (+15%), at 4096 314 -> 385 (+23%), at 8192
  240 -> 341 (+42%); tg32 unchanged (11.05 / 11.22, at 4096 10.40 / 10.29).
- the full-size SWA cache NanoLLM runs (two interleaved rounds): empty context 416-458 -> 447-472, at 4096
  315-319 -> 385-394 (+22%), at 8192 241-242 -> 346-347 (+43%). The matmul attention on the sliding-window layers
  too: 300-305 at 4096, 223-228 at 8192, worse than flash attention.

NanoLLM gemma.json on that iGPU (5 requests, prompts of 0.7k-4.6k tokens, MTP with 2 drafts), two interleaved
rounds: wall 95.7-97.9 -> 90.0-92.1 s, prefill 40.3-41.9 -> 36.5-38.5 s. The compute buffer grows by the KQ
matrix of one layer (peak GPU memory of the process 3.47-3.50 -> 3.62 GB at n_ctx 8192).

KL divergence on Vulkan against flash attention everywhere (llama-perplexity -c 2048 -ub 512, 6 chunks of a
local ru/en text): small_batch mean 0.00113, 99.9% 0.030, same top token 98.2%; flash attention off (the matmul
attention everywhere) 0.00132 / 0.039 / 98.0%; the CPU backend 0.00238 / 0.043 / 98.0%.
